bject.\n2. Choose axes. Resolve each force into components.\n3. Write equations: \\( \\sum Fx = m ax \\) and \\( \\sum Fy = m ay \\).\n4. Insert known values. Solve for the unknown acceleration or force.\n5. Example: 5 kg box. Pull 20 N right. Friction 5 N left.\n- Net force: \\( \\sum F_x = 20 - 5 = 15 \text{ N} \\).\n- Acceleration: \\( a_x = 15/5 = 3 \text{ m/s}^2 \\) right.\n- Check: \\( 15 \text{ N} = 5 \text{ kg} \\cdot 3 \text{ m/s}^2 \\).\n- Left side = Right side\n4. Real-World Example\nPushing a grocery cart: a stronger push gives faster acceleration. A heavier cart accelerates less for the same push, consistent with \\( \\sum F = m a \\).\n5. Pro Tip\nAlways draw the free-body diagram and set axes. Mass is not weight. You got this!","raw_text":"1. Concept Overview\nNewton's second law links force to acceleration: net force equals mass times acceleration. It predicts motion and underpins free-body problems, a frequent exam staple.\n\n2. Key Formulas\n\\[ \\sum \\vec{F} = m \\vec{a} \\]\n\\[ \\sum F_x = m a_x,\\quad \\sum F_y = m a_y \\]\n\\[ \\vec{F}_{\\mathrm{net}} = \\frac{d\\vec{p}}{dt} \\]\n\n3. Step-by-Step\n1. Draw a clear free-body diagram for the object.\n2. Choose axes. Resolve each force into components.\n3. Write equations: \\( \\sum F_x = m a_x \\) and \\( \\sum F_y = m a_y \\).\n4. Insert known values. Solve for the unknown acceleration or force.\n5. Example: 5 kg box. Pull 20 N right. Friction 5 N left.\n- Net force: \\( \\sum F_x = 20 - 5 = 15 \\text{ N} \\).\n- Acceleration: \\( a_x = 15/5 = 3 \\text{ m/s}^2 \\) right.\n- Check: \\( 15 \\text{ N} = 5 \\text{ kg} \\cdot 3 \\text{ m/s}^2 \\).\n- ✓ Left side = Right side\n\n4. Real-World Example\nPushing a grocery cart: a stronger push gives faster acceleration. A heavier cart accelerates less for the same push, consistent with \\( \\sum F = m a \\).\n\n5. Pro Tip\nAlways draw the free-body diagram and set axes. Mass is not weight. You got this!","confidence":0.95,"progressive_sections":{"foun